---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import { celestial, photos, scenes } from '../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../components/CTABand.astro';
const crumbs = [{ label: 'Approach', href: '/approach/' }];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="My Approach: how I read a chart"
  description="How Mo Lumen practices astrology: technically grounded, synthesis-oriented, conversational, and centered on the client's lived experience and choices."
  path="/approach/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">How I work</p>
        <h1>How I read a chart</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Technique matters. So do context, curiosity, conversation, and the person actually living
          the chart.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.approachPhilosophy.src}
        srcset={scenes.approachPhilosophy.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.approachPhilosophy.alt}
        width={scenes.approachPhilosophy.width}
        height={scenes.approachPhilosophy.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <figure class="portrait-card portrait-card--inline">
        <img src={photos.moApproach.src} alt={photos.moApproach.alt} width={photos.moApproach.width} height={photos.moApproach.height} loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Mo Lumen in her studio</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2>The whole chart, not isolated labels</h2>
      <p>
        Astrology gives us a remarkable number of pieces to work with: planets, signs, houses,
        aspects, condition, timing, and more. Knowing what each piece can mean is important. The
        art is synthesis: seeing which pieces are speaking most strongly, how they modify one
        another, and what kind of pattern begins to emerge when you stop looking at them one at a
        time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Even then, a chart is not a finished biography. The same configuration can show up in more
        than one way. I may see a path that looks important and ask whether it matches what you are
        actually experiencing. Your answer is not an interruption to the reading; it is part of
        how the reading becomes more specific to you.
      </p>
      <h2>A conversation, not a performance</h2>
      <p>
        You do not need to arrive knowing astrology, and you do not need to be especially talkative.
        I will guide the reading, explain what I see, and make room for your questions and feedback
        as we go. Some clients have a lot to say right away. Others need time to listen before they
        know what they want to add. Both are completely workable.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.galaxyTidepool} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Technique, intuition, and questions</h2>
      <p>
        I have studied across astrological methods and traditions, including horary and traditional
        techniques as well as modern and other contemporary approaches. I use the tools that help
        me understand the chart and the question in front of me rather than asking every chart to
        conform to one school.
      </p>
      <p>
        Intuition is part of how I notice. It may point my attention toward a possibility or a
        question worth asking, but I do not treat an intuitive impression as unquestionable fact.
        It belongs in the same conversation as the chart and your lived experience. Intuition can
        suggest where to look; it does not end the inquiry.
      </p>
      <h2>Fate and choice, both</h2>
      <p>
        I use astrology to look at conditions, tendencies, seasons, and timing. It can widen the
        view around a decision or help name a pattern that has been difficult to see from the
        inside. It does not cast your vote, take your job, choose your partner, or make your next
        move. There is fate and there is choice, and I use astrology to create perspective rather
        than deliver a verdict.
      </p>
      <h2>Candor with compassion</h2>
      <p>
        I will tell you what I see, including the parts that may be uncomfortable or complicated.
        Difficult configurations are not punishments, and awareness does not magically make them
        easy. It can, however, give you more room to recognize a pattern, understand the choices
        around it, and decide how you want to meet it. Expect honesty delivered kindly, and kindness
        that does not dodge the truth.
      </p>
      <h2>What I won't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No fear-based predictions, doom timelines, or "the stars say you must."</li>
        <li>No false certainty. Some branches of astrology, including horary, are designed to answer a specific question directly. When I use them, I will be clear about what the method supports rather than promise an outcome astrology cannot responsibly guarantee.</li>
        <li>No medical, psychological, legal, or financial advice; those belong with licensed professionals, and I'll say so.</li>
        <li>No pressure to book, rebook, agree with me, or continue.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Ethics, in practice</h2>
      <p>
        Your birth data and everything you share in a session are treated as confidential and
        sensitive. I follow the ethical standards of the professional organizations I belong to
        and serve, NCGR, OPA, and ISAR, and readings are for adults 18 and over. The
        <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a> and <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> spell out
        the details in plain language.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Bring me your life and your question."
    body="I'll bring the chart, the preparation, and what I'm noticing. Before you decide to book, see exactly how that conversation works."
    buttonLabel="How Readings Work"
    buttonHref="/how-readings-work/"
    secondaryLabel="Not sure which reading would fit? Try the Reading Finder"
    secondaryHref="/reading-finder/"
  />
</BaseLayout>